<span id="S3.T1.2.2.2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">interstitial</span></span>
<span id="S3.T1.2.2.2.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.2.2.2.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">cystitis</span></span>
</span></span><span id="S3.T1.2.2.2.3" class="ltx_text"></span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">1</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T1.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span> <span id="S3.T1.2.3.1.2" class="ltx_text">
<span id="S3.T1.2.3.1.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<span id="S3.T1.2.3.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.2.3.1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">acute</span></span>
<span id="S3.T1.2.3.1.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.2.3.1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">pancreatitis</span></span>
</span></span><span id="S3.T1.2.3.1.3" class="ltx_text"></span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T1.2.3.2.1" class="ltx_text"></span> <span id="S3.T1.2.3.2.2" class="ltx_text">
<span id="S3.T1.2.3.2.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<span id="S3.T1.2.3.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.2.3.2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">chronic</span></span>
<span id="S3.T1.2.3.2.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.2.3.2.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">pancreatitis</span></span>
<span id="S3.T1.2.3.2.2.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.2.3.2.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">exacerbation</span></span>
</span></span><span id="S3.T1.2.3.2.3" class="ltx_text"></span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">0</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T1.2.4.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span> <span id="S3.T1.2.4.1.2" class="ltx_text">
<span id="S3.T1.2.4.1.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<span id="S3.T1.2.4.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.2.4.1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">rotavirus</span></span>
<span id="S3.T1.2.4.1.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.2.4.1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">gastroenteritis</span></span>
</span></span><span id="S3.T1.2.4.1.3" class="ltx_text"></span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T1.2.4.2.1" class="ltx_text"></span> <span id="S3.T1.2.4.2.2" class="ltx_text">
<span id="S3.T1.2.4.2.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<span id="S3.T1.2.4.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.2.4.2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">acute</span></span>
<span id="S3.T1.2.4.2.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.2.4.2.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">intestinal</span></span>
<span id="S3.T1.2.4.2.2.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.2.4.2.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">infection</span></span>
<span id="S3.T1.2.4.2.2.1.4" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.2.4.2.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">gastroenteritis</span></span>
</span></span><span id="S3.T1.2.4.2.3" class="ltx_text"></span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">1</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T1.2.5.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span> <span id="S3.T1.2.5.1.2" class="ltx_text">
<span id="S3.T1.2.5.1.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<span id="S3.T1.2.5.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.2.5.1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">foodborne</span></span>
<span id="S3.T1.2.5.1.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.2.5.1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">toxicosis</span></span>
</span></span><span id="S3.T1.2.5.1.3" class="ltx_text"></span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T1.2.5.2.1" class="ltx_text"></span> <span id="S3.T1.2.5.2.2" class="ltx_text">
<span id="S3.T1.2.5.2.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<span id="S3.T1.2.5.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.2.5.2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">viral infection</span></span>
</span></span><span id="S3.T1.2.5.2.3" class="ltx_text"></span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">0</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.2.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T1.2.6.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span> <span id="S3.T1.2.6.1.2" class="ltx_text">
<span id="S3.T1.2.6.1.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<span id="S3.T1.2.6.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.2.6.1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">arrhythmia</span></span>
</span></span><span id="S3.T1.2.6.1.3" class="ltx_text"></span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T1.2.6.2.1" class="ltx_text"></span> <span id="S3.T1.2.6.2.2" class="ltx_text">
<span id="S3.T1.2.6.2.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<span id="S3.T1.2.6.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.2.6.2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">functional</span></span>
<span id="S3.T1.2.6.2.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.2.6.2.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">heart rhythm</span></span>
<span id="S3.T1.2.6.2.2.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.2.6.2.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">disorders syndrome</span></span>
</span></span><span id="S3.T1.2.6.2.3" class="ltx_text"></span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">1</td></tr>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A key part of the experiment is to calculate the values of functions <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>χ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> in eqs. 1 and 2. As the algorithm <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mi>A</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{A}</annotation></semantics></math> and the expert <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mi>E</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{E}</annotation></semantics></math> responses are text strings, how can we find which of pairs <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d^{A},d^{E})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mi>A</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mi>E</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d^{A},d^{E})</annotation></semantics></math> do match or not? Strings do not have to be identical to contain the same diagnosis, see Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.2 Match function ‣ 3 Methods ‣ How to Evaluate Medical AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. 
<br class="ltx_break">To solve this problem, let <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{F}</annotation></semantics></math> be a set of pairs of text strings. Then any match function should be <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M:\mathfrak{F}\mapsto\mathbb{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>𝔉</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mi>𝔹</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M:\mathfrak{F}\mapsto\mathbb{B}</annotation></semantics></math>. Now we can calculate the multiplicity <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> and the characteristic <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>χ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi</annotation></semantics></math> functions for diagnoses in case of <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|D^{A}|~=~|D^{E}|~=~k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>A</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.330em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.608em">=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>E</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.330em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.608em">=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|D^{A}|~=~|D^{E}|~=~k</annotation></semantics></math> as long as we have a proper definition for a match function:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(D^{A},D^{E})=\sum_{p=1}^{k}\sum_{q=1}^{k}M(d^{A}_{p},d^{E}_{q})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>A</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>E</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>A</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>q</mi><mi>E</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(D^{A},D^{E})=\sum_{p=1}^{k}\sum_{q=1}^{k}M(d^{A}_{p},d^{E}_{q})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(D^{A},D^{E})=\begin{cases}1;&amp;\mu(D^{A},D^{E})&gt;0\\
0;&amp;\mu(D^{A},D^{E})=0\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>A</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>E</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>;</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>A</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>E</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>;</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>A</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>E</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(D^{A},D^{E})=\begin{cases}1;&amp;\mu(D^{A},D^{E})&gt;0\\
0;&amp;\mu(D^{A},D^{E})=0\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To this end, we implemented a supervised meta-modeling approach based on comprehensive feature engineering. The resulting model, used to define the match function <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, predicts whether a pair of diagnoses <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d^{A},d^{E})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mi>A</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mi>E</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d^{A},d^{E})</annotation></semantics></math> should be considered a match.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Feature Construction and Preprocessing</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The match function was trained on labeled diagnosis pairs through a multi-stage pipeline. First, we applied preprocessing to normalize the data, including lowercasing, punctuation removal, abbreviation expansion, and domain-specific rule corrections. After preprocessing, the train and test sets comprised 4,833 and 1,469 samples, respectively. The train and test sets were curated by 3 medical experts: The final value of the label was decided by a vote of each of them. Another expert additionally reviewed the test dataset in accordance with the criteria developed by the same experts together. The criteria developed by experts to assess the similarity of diagnoses are shown in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS1" title="A.1 Direct prompt for LLM ‣ Appendix A LLM Prompts Used ‣ How to Evaluate Medical AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a>. An example of a dataset can be seen in Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.2 Match function ‣ 3 Methods ‣ How to Evaluate Medical AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSSx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To train the meta-model, we extracted 17 features for each pair, grouped into four categories:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSSx1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Direct LLM prompting:</span> Language models were directly instructed to assess diagnosis similarity (see <a href="#A1.SS1" title="A.1 Direct prompt for LLM ‣ Appendix A LLM Prompts Used ‣ How to Evaluate Medical AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a> for the prompt). The same criteria developed by expert physicians that guided them in manually marking up the dataset were used to write the prompt. Five models (Llama-3.1-70B-Instruct, Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct, GigaChat-Max, Vikhr-Nemo-12B-Instruct, Phi-4) where selected out of 20
by the highest F1 score on the test dataset.
Each model’s output contributed a binary feature indicating whether it considered the pair a match.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLM with RAG and ICD dictionary:</span> We used a Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) system indexed on 20,000+ ICD entries. Each diagnosis was matched to ICD code using an LLM-guided retrieval process. We generated an embedding for each entry in the ICD-10 library using the BERGAMOT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sakhovskiy et al. (2024)</a></cite> model,
stored in FAISS
vector store <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Douze et al. (2024)</a></cite> for Euclidean distance-based similarity search. For each input diagnosis, a direct match was first attempted. If no direct mapping was found, the top 15 most similar ICD-10 entries were retrieved using vector similarity.
Two LLMs (Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct and Vikhr-Nemo-12B-Instruct) were then employed separately in different experiments to identify the most relevant ICD entry from the retrieved candidates (see <a href="#A1.SS2" title="A.2 Prompt for LLM with RAG and ICD dictionary ‣ Appendix A LLM Prompts Used ‣ How to Evaluate Medical AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a> for the prompt). The input diagnosis was subsequently assigned the ICD code corresponding to the model’s selected entry.
Two diagnoses were considered a match if their ICD codes shared the same first three characters, contributing a binary feature to the meta-model.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Text embedding similarity:</span> We used three embedding models: Giga, BERGAMOT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sakhovskiy et al. (2024)</a></cite>, and Arctic-Embed <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Merrick et al. (2024)</a></cite> to compute cosine similarity between diagnosis vectors. These scores served as continuous features.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Linguistic similarity ratios:</span> For the fourth group of features, we computed linguistic similarity ratios between pairs of diagnoses. For each diagnosis, we extracted sets of unigrams, bigrams, stems, lemmas, and part-of-speech (POS) tags. Similarity ratios were calculated as the size of the intersection between corresponding sets divided by the minimum size of the sets. In addition, we computed the normalized Levenshtein distance between the diagnosis strings to capture character-level differences.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSSx2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Meta-Model Training and Evaluation</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Model selection and training were performed using the AutoGluon AutoML framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Erickson et al. (2020)</a></cite>. Optimization targeted average precision on cross-validation folds. Training was constrained to a maximum of one hour with automatic ensembling (stacking) enabled. The resulting best-performing model was a
bagged ensemble of LightGBM classifier as a base model.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSSx3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Feature Importance and Selection</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We initially filtered features using correlation analysis (see Fig. <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2.3 The Path to Stable Metrics ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ How to Evaluate Medical AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), excluding low-informative candidates such as 2-gram and Part-of-speech ratios, but observed no performance gain. Final selection was based on permutation-shuffling feature importance provided by AutoGluon (see Fig. <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Feature Importance and Selection ‣ 3.2 Match function ‣ 3 Methods ‣ How to Evaluate Medical AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), which indicated all features contributed non-negatively. Additionaly, the SHAP values analysis (see <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Feature Importance and Selection ‣ 3.2 Match function ‣ 3 Methods ‣ How to Evaluate Medical AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) validated the importance of each feature by providing a game theory-based measure. Hence, the full feature set was retained for deployment.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.11941v1/fig/meta-modeling-test-feature-importance-log.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:215/141;" width="215" height="141" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Meta-model (the match function <math id="S3.F2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>) features importance by permutation-shuffling.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.11941v1/fig/meta-modeling-test-shap-200.png" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:215/216;" width="215" height="216" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Meta-model (the match function <math id="S3.F3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>) features importance by SHAP values.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSSx3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSSx3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Since we introduce custom match function, they have their own quality metrics. The problem of quality assessment of such a function reduces to a binary classification problem, and we use standard metrics for it: precision, recall, <math id="S3.SS2.SSSx3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-measure, and accuracy. Table <a href="#S3.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ Feature Importance and Selection ‣ 3.2 Match function ‣ 3 Methods ‣ How to Evaluate Medical AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> reports the evaluation metrics for the final match function <math id="S3.SS2.SSSx3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, which underpins the computation of <math id="S3.SS2.SSSx3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSSx3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>χ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi</annotation></semantics></math> in our diagnostic comparison experiments.
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span> Match functions metrics</figcaption>
<table id="S3.T2.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S3.T2.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T2.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Precision</span></td>
<td id="S3.T2.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T2.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Recall</span></td>
<td id="S3.T2.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><math id="S3.T2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mn>𝟏</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S3.T2.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T2.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Accuracy</span></td>
<td id="S3.T2.2.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T2.2.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Support</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T2.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T2.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">0.91</td>
<td id="S3.T2.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">0.90</td>
<td id="S3.T2.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">0.91</td>
<td id="S3.T2.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">0.98</td>
<td id="S3.T2.2.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">1469</td></tr>
</table>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Results and Discussion</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Data</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The raw dataset is a collection of <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=360" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>360</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=360</annotation></semantics></math> chats. The first half of the dataset consists of chats from two experts played out a pre-determined scenario. One of them knew the disease and their symptoms and was the patient. The other tried to define the disease by asking questions as the physician. The second half of the dataset consists of the chats between the actor playing a patient and LLM playing a physician. Ground truth labels for the dataset were marked by <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z=7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=7</annotation></semantics></math> experts, all of them are resident physicians. Each expert marked up all chats with <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{max}=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{max}=3</annotation></semantics></math> diagnoses. Also did the algorithm. 
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>LLM comparison</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Based on the data from experts, we calculated pairwise and relative metrics for the diagnostic algorithm using different LLMs. For comparison, we selected the LLMs that show the best metrics on the MERA dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fenogenova et al. (2024)</a></cite> (see Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.2 LLM comparison ‣ 4 Results and Discussion ‣ How to Evaluate Medical AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span> Relative quality of the algorithmic diagnostics for different LLMs </figcaption>
